  "query_preview": "/Monday through Friday/... \nThe overall pattern heading into the first half of next week is \nlikely to become more favorable for more widespread convective \nactivity across the region, including for the lower deserts. The \nsubtropical high is projected to weaken even further and migrate \nmore towards the Four Corners Region, resulting in the overall \nflow to shift more out of the southeast. This will cause moisture \nvalues to increase with the latest EPS and GEFS indicating PWATs \nsettling around 1.5-1.8\" and low-level mixing ratios exceeding \n11-12 g/kg. This increase in moisture will result in better \ninstability and weaker convective inhibition, making the \nenvironment more favorable for storms that do develop across the \ntypical higher terrain areas to survive into the lower deserts. \nThis more favorable environmental setup is depicted in the latest \nforecast from the NBM, which shows PoPs above 40% across the \nsouth-central AZ lower deserts starting on Monday with elevated \nchances continuing through the middle of the week. Which day early\nnext week could be the most active is difficult to pinpoint at \nthis time as it will be dictated by mesoscale processes that are \ndifficult to predict this far out. \n\nHeading towards the latter half of the week, latest guidance \nindicates the subtropical high migrating further eastward towards \nTexas while an upper-level low sets up off the California coast. \nThis overall setup is likely to shift the flow more out of the \nsouthwest, bringing in some drier air as PWATs drop under 1.5\". \nAs a result, an overall downtrend in storm activity is likely by \nthe end of the week.\n\nWith the weakening of the subtropical high and in increase in \nconvective activity and cloud cover, temperatures next week are \nexpected to gradually cool with afternoon highs expected to fall to \nnear seasonal levels by Tuesday with afternoon highs of 102-108 \ndegrees across the lower deserts. The NBM is even showing \ntemperatures potentially cooling even further through the end of the \nweek as it shows afternoon highs of 100-106 degrees, which if it \nmaterializes, would be slightly below normal for the middle of \nAugust.",
